Monday morning in the business district  of Amanda it's a sad sight whole  neighborhoods of the biggest city in the  region have come to a standstill most  businesses remain closed green grocer  Roberts isn't opening his shop today  even though he badly needs to it is a  very difficult situation to imagine that  on a deal like this some of us will live  on a daily bread meaning that today  there is no bread today there is no  break but we adapt because it's a course  that were fighting for Robert says he  hasn't seen any development in the  region even though he regularly pays his  taxes he and others are protesting by  not opening their businesses and they  are also calling for the release of a  number of demonstrators in the mostly  english-speaking West of Cameroon many  people feel marginalized they feel  they've been forgotten by President Paul  BR and the french-speaking majority of  the country in late 2016 lawyers took to  the streets to protests teachers soon  followed suit then finally people from  all walks of life the authorities  responded with a heavy hand at least six  people died and dozens were arrested  internet access in the region was  blocked for three months we try to talk  to the governor here about the situation  but he didn't grant us an interview a  government spokesperson told us on the  phone that actions are more important  than words and that they are working on  the situation but many people we talk to  here are complaining that there is no  serious dialog happening instead parts  of the population become more radical  businesses owned by people who did not  take part in the protest are often the  target of arson attacks we talked to a  young man who wants to remain anonymous  he himself was almost arrested he wants  the region to become independent and he  thinks the use of violence is legitimate  because he doesn't think the government  would allow a referendum  it's a necessary evil I wouldn't say  citing people's properties on fire is a  good thing but it's a necessary evil as  of now if I have to tell you everything  that runs through my head I might look  like a terrorist to you but I'm not a  terrorist  but when you look at the frustration  when the frustration mounts to a certain  extent you cannot contain you become  radical that's what happens we're just  hoping that sometime soon somebody will  come and say here are the guns you need  take them and fight for your freedom and  we will do just that  the mood in the city is tense divisions  between radicals and moderates have  widened the church has tried to mediate  and is calling on all parties to engage  in a dialogue the mine or the question  of the church is very clear that  violence is not to be encouraged but at  the same time the church stands for  justice  that people should be treated according  to their rights they should be given  fair hearing and that they should be  able to be listened to and if they have  difficulties of grievances that these  could be looked into so that we can have  a lasting solution Roberts the  greengrocer is also hoping that there  will be a peaceful resolution and that  the government finally responds schools  have been closed for almost a year now  and his kids have been affected Robert  says the current situation cannot  continue
 

